# Resonance - percussion by what a thing is, voiced against the debug build.
#
# **A patch here is a kit, not a sound.** Eight pads, each a bank of
# resonators tuned to the modes of a shape, struck somewhere with something
# and left to ring. The note picks the pad; there is no pitch to play.
#
# So this bank is written as a *library of objects* - a conga, a woodblock,
# an anvil, a singing bowl - and each kit is a chosen set of eight. That is
# what makes two kits sound like two kits rather than two accidents: a conga
# is the same conga wherever it turns up.
#
# **The six shapes are six sets of mode ratios, and they are the whole
# instrument:**
#
#   Membrane  1, 1.594, 2.136, 2.296 - Bessel, and so *not* a pitch. Why a
#             tom is a thump and a conga is a note-ish thump.
#   Bar       1, 2.756, 5.404, 8.933 - a free bar. The huge gap to the second
#             mode is why wood sounds like wood.
#   Plate     1, 2.04, 3.01, 3.66 - dense and close-packed, which is a crash.
#   Tube      the harmonic series, and so the only shape here with a true
#             pitch. Bottles, pipes, chimes.
#   Bowl      1, 2, 3.01, 4.17 - a bell, including the minor third that
#             founders spent four centuries tuning out.
#   Metal     the square roots - 1, 1.414, 1.732 - which never agree with
#             each other or with anything else.
#
# `inharm` raises every ratio to the power (1 + it), so a *negative* value
# pulls a shape towards harmonic: that is the tuning paste on a tabla and the
# hammering on a steel pan, and it is why both have a pitch when the shapes
# they are made of do not.
#
# `hit` is where it is struck, and it nulls the modes with a node there -
# strike a bar halfway along and the even modes vanish, which is a thumb
# piano. `hard` is the mallet.
#
# **And the pads hear each other.** `coupling` feeds each pad's output into
# all the others, so a kit rings as one object rather than eight islands -
# hit the kick and the snare buzzes, which every real kit does and no drum
# machine bothers with. `Ringing` and `Cathedral` are built to show it.
#
# Families in tab order, plainest to strangest within each: drum 10 (Init
# first), hand 8, wood 8, mallet 8, metal 8, bell 8, glass 7 and found 7.
# Every kit but Init carries eight named macros. Big Room, Tam Tams, Temple
# Bowls, Glass Bowls, Chandelier and Radiator send to the song's reverb and
# Floorboards a little.
#
# The newer kits list their eight objects in their own comment, pad by pad,
# and an object that turns up in two kits is the same recipe in both. Pad
# drive is normalised on the level a pad reaches, so it adds grit and
# sustain at about the same peak.
#
# Every kit but Init peaks at about -11 dB.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Resonance` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Resonance
# Two demos: `mallets` plays all eight pads up and down with a roll and two
# struck together, which is what a tuned kit wants; `beat` is a drum pattern
# for the kits that are drums. `beat` uses seven of the ten voices it was
# written for, so on an eight-pad machine three pads never sound - hence the
# other one.
role    mallets

patch Init family=drum

